WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.600
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.980
Animated movies create the impression that characters

00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:14.363
are moving smoothly. But if you slow them down enough, you'll realize there are lots

00:00:14.363 --> 00:00:19.103
of individual images. Each image is just a little bit different from the one before.

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:23.967
Speed them back up, and your eyes are tricked into thinking you're watching real life motion.

00:00:25.407 --> 00:00:27.967
A very simple example of this effect is a flipbook.

00:00:28.847 --> 00:00:29.887
The key to animation,

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:34.951
whether on paper or the computer, is that part of the drawing is changing over time.

00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:39.935
An easy way to achieve a change is to use the randomNumber

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:47.215
command to generate a new random location, size, or color for the shape we're drawing.

00:00:47.215 --> 00:00:49.919
Here's a simple program that draws a single rectangle

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:53.759
with a random x position and a random y position.

00:00:53.759 --> 00:00:56.879
If we run and reset this program over and over again,

00:00:56.879 --> 00:00:58.599
we can see that it draws the rectangle

00:00:58.599 --> 00:00:59.903
in a new position each time.

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:04.983
But that's like a flipbook with only one page.

00:01:04.983 --> 00:01:09.783
To create real animations, we need to be able to string these pages of the flipbook together.

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:14.887
In Game Lab, we'll do this using the draw function.

00:01:14.887 --> 00:01:17.727
The draw function is special in Game Lab.

00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:19.767
The commands inside of the draw function are run

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:24.591
line by line from top to bottom. But once the computer reaches the end of the commands

00:01:24.591 --> 00:01:28.671
inside the draw function, it jumps back to the beginning and repeats the commands over

00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:29.855
and over again, as long as

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.495
your program is running.

00:01:32.495 --> 00:01:36.415
Because the code in the draw function repeats in a loop, we'll just refer to this as the

00:01:36.415 --> 00:01:39.375
draw loop.

00:01:39.375 --> 00:01:39.815
Let's move our

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:43.999
rectangle inside the draw loop and see what happens.

00:01:43.999 --> 00:01:47.559
Whoa, so many rectangles!

00:01:47.559 --> 00:01:49.823
We can see that a rectangle isn't just being drawn once

00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:54.503
anymore. Game Lab is automatically calling the commands inside of the draw loop

00:01:54.503 --> 00:01:59.743
many times a second. In this program, that means that a new random x and y

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:04.527
location is generated and then a rectangle is drawn over and over and over.

00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:09.567
You can place as many commands as you like inside of the draw function.

00:02:09.791 --> 00:02:16.791
For example, let's choose a new fill color before drawing each rectangle by placing a fill command inside the draw loop.

00:02:16.791 --> 00:02:19.775
Instead of always choosing the same color, we'll randomize by

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:23.535
replacing the red value with the randomNumber command.

00:02:23.535 --> 00:02:25.695
So in every frame of the flipbook,

00:02:25.695 --> 00:02:27.735
the computer is picking a new shade of red

00:02:27.735 --> 00:02:29.535
and setting that as the fill color.

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:33.679
Then it picks a new x and y value and draws a rectangle with those values.

00:02:35.039 --> 00:02:39.439
In a typical animated movie or flipbook, you don't continue to see all of the drawings that

00:02:39.439 --> 00:02:39.679
came

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:44.943
earlier in the animation. Instead, you have the illusion of a single shape or character moving

00:02:44.943 --> 00:02:49.503
over time. You'll discover how to make that happen with the draw loop soon.

00:02:49.727 --> 00:02:52.307
[MUSIC]
